# Project Constitution and Master Engineering Prompt

The supreme governing document is `docs/PROJECT_CONSTITUTION.md`. Read it first. Obey the constitutions. Preserve architecture. Identify root causes. Validate before completion. Never mark work complete without evidence.

Before any sprint, implementation, review, refactor, bug fix, diagnosis, or certification, read `docs/engineering/MASTER_ENGINEERING_PROMPT.md`. That file is the authoritative engineering standard. Follow it by default. Unexplained failures, corrections, and certification follow **§10**.

Then use `docs/engineering/README.md` (Engineering Index) for the Foundation Runbook, architecture, coding standards, branch strategy, release process, and sprint process.

- Never continue on an unhealthy foundation. Run the pre-flight checklist first.
- One logical change at a time. Reuse existing architecture. Never bypass validation.
- Find the root cause, explain why it occurred, and permanently prevent recurrence. Do not patch symptoms.
- Do not redesign Runtime, IDS, Capability Registry, Definition Registry, persistence, or Executive Environments unless that is the named programme.
- Never tell the founder a task is complete until it has been verified in the running application.

## Operating checklist (MEP §10)

- Cursor executes inspection, commands, tests, diagnostics, and read-only Git. Do not send the founder through routine terminal sequences.
- Planning-first (Engineering Constitution §2A): before modifying files,
  require a proportionate Founder-approved implementation or correction
  packet and verify its material assumptions read-only against the live
  repository. If repository evidence materially contradicts it, preserve
  the tree, stop, report evidence, and return for reconciliation; do not
  invent scope or architecture.
- Unexplained failure: **read-only diagnosis first**. Report ROOT CAUSE, EVIDENCE, OWNERSHIP (A Venture / B VentureOS / C test-infra / D interaction), SMALLEST SAFE CORRECTION, REBUILD REQUIRED, REGRESSION RISK, CERTIFICATION IMPACT, CONFIDENCE.
- Stop for founder authorisation when the correction touches shared architecture, certified behaviour, persistence/schema/runtime, rebuild, or material regression risk.
- One hypothesis, one minimal correction. MEDIUM/LOW confidence is a hypothesis under verification. If it fails, return to diagnosis — do not stack fixes.
- Verify targeted test → related domain → broader regression → full suite → certification. Targeted-first does **not** waive full-suite sprint completion.
- A failing, hanging, cancelled, or unexplained verification gate **stops** that run. Do not silently repair in verification-only mode.
- Before staging: classify dirty files A–E. Unexpected files block staging. Never `git add .` or `git add -A`. Exact-path staging only when authorised.
- Certification evidence must include assertions, process exit, failures, cancellations, skipped tests, warnings, exact commands, and working-tree provenance. Passing assertions with a hang or non-zero exit are not a clean pass.
